% In this report I plot Velocity vs Amplitude for each device, 
% (1) real sense, (2) real sense+ visage, (3) Smart eye, (4) smart eye+ visage (when ready)
% According to Murray, the high correlation in linear relationship between amplitude & velocity is the prime 
% determinant of the JDS algorithm. 

% The subjects recorded using real sense are different from the subjects recorded by smart eye.

% Two files needed to subset to get the amplitude and velocity for the blinks
% file name : "..blinks", In "blinks" gives information on TRUE blinks only
% record start+2 = starting row number; end+2 = end row number
 
% subset "time domain" file using starting row numbers & ending row numbers 
% Plot S_smoothed EM = Amplitude & S_Velocity = velocity


\section{Introduction}

The linear relationship between amplitude and velocity is the backbone of the JDS algorithm. The measurement obtained from glasses usually hold this relaitonship but measurements from other devices, like Smart Eye and Real Sense is yet to examined. This report examined  the linear relationship using scatter plots and Perason Correlation coefficient between amplitude and velocity measured by different devices. To understand the behaviour of a video recording device, a comparison of the linear relationship between a video recording device and glasses measurement obtained within a same recording session also carried out. So far we have used two video systems, Smart Eye and Real Sense, and an image processing system, Visage. We have data collected over following combinations:

\begin{itemize}
\item Smart Eye
\item Real Sense
\item Real Sense + Visage processing
\end{itemize}


It is also noted that the linear relarionship between amplitude and velocity holds for only events categorised as `saccades' and `blinks'. Since saccades can not be categorised from video recording, in this report only events caegorised as `blinks' are included. however, they does not have to be `blink correct shape'. 


\begin{itemize}
\item Relationship between Amplitude and Velocity
\item Relaitonship between ZCI and BTD separated for alert and impaired sessions
\item Relationship between BECD and BTD separated for alert and impaired sessions

\end{itemize}


\section{SmartEye}

The recordings from Smart Eye has gone through many steps of processing to obtain signal as close to the corresponding glasses signal. In this report the sessions available in the folder ``T:\textbackslash 6-SW\textbackslash OptalertVideo\textbackslash JTV\_original" are used and decisions to which sessions to be used is available i nthe Decisions.tx file in the same folder.


\subsection{Relationship betwen Amplitude and Velocity: Individual session}

<<smarteye_decision, cache=TRUE, echo=FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE>>=

# root directory for smarteye 
sm_root_dir <- "T:/6-SW/OptalertVideo/JTV_original" 

# read the Decisions.txt file
sm_decisions <-  read.csv("T:/6-SW/OptalertVideo/JTV_original/Decisions.txt", header=F)
# Identify "in" session accordign to Decisions.txt
sm_decisions[,1] <- as.character(sm_decisions[,1])
tmp <- strsplit(sm_decisions[,1], "==")
sm_decisions[,2] <- trimws(do.call(c, lapply(tmp, "[", 1)), which = "both")
sm_decisions[,3] <- trimws(do.call(c, lapply(tmp, "[", 2)), which = "both")
colnames(sm_decisions) <- c("name","session","decision")

# use session with "in" decisions
sm_in <- sm_decisions$session[sm_decisions$decision == "IN"]
# remove NA entries
sm_in <- sm_in[!is.na(sm_in)] 


# Get folder name for all sessions available in the sm_root_dir
sm_session_all <- paste0(sm_root_dir,"/", sm_decisions$session)
# manual fix: delete 3 sessions NO information available even though happeared in the Decisions.txt
sm_session_all <- sm_session_all[! basename(sm_session_all) %in% c("20160809_1800_tm", "20160810_0300_tm", "20160810_0400_tm")]

# Get folder name for IN session 
sm_session_in <- paste0(sm_root_dir,"/", sm_in)


# device name
sm_device <- c("glasses", "smarteye")


@



<<sm_jtv, cache=TRUE ,echo=FALSE,warning=FALSE, mesage=FALSE, fig.align='center', fig.show='asis', fig.height = 10>>=

# Store percentage calculated from JTV
sm_jtv <- list()

# store perminute n_lapsing and  n_stimuli 
sm_pm_lapsing <- list()

#Store blink data from glasses and video
sm_dt <- list()

#Store slope, corr, n from glasses and video condition on type
sm_stat <- list()

# session to work with. If only IN sessions to work OR all sessions to work with. 
sm_work_session <- sm_session_in


#################### Start looping ##########################
for( s in 1:length(sm_work_session))
{
  #########################  JTV stuff ##################
  jtv_path <- list.files(sm_work_session[s], pattern = "_jtv_", full.names = TRUE)
  
  react <- read.csv(list.files(jtv_path, pattern="ReactionTimes.csv", full.names = TRUE),
                        header=TRUE, 
                        stringsAsFactors = FALSE, 
                        check.names = FALSE)
 
  # allocate the state depending on the entry
  react$state <- 0    #"not_lapsing"
  react$state[react[,3]=="---"] <- 1  #"lapsing"
  
  # Calculate the minute start_time and end_time from the Time column
  nr <- nrow(react)
  lastmin <- ceiling(react[nr,2]/60)
  start_time <- seq(0, lastmin*60, by=60)
  end_time <- start_time+59.998
  window <- seq(from = 0, to = (length(start_time)-1), by = 1)
  
  ####################  per minute reaction output  ######################
  react_pm <-  data.frame(session = basename(sm_work_session[s]),
                          decision = toupper(sm_decisions[sm_decisions$session == basename(sm_work_session[s]), "decision"]), 
                          start_time = start_time, 
                          end_time=end_time, 
                          window = window,
                          n_stimuli = 0,
                          n_lapses = 0,
                          stringsAsFactors = FALSE)
  
 # For each minute : count the number of stimuli & number of missing
  for( i in 2:nrow(react_pm))
  {
    tmp <- react[react[,2]> start_time[i-1] &  react[,2]<= start_time[i],]
    react_pm$n_stimuli[i] <- nrow(tmp)
    react_pm$n_lapses[i] <- sum(tmp$state)
  }
  # calculate JTV percentage n_lapses/n_stimuli for (1) whole 20 min session, and (2) last 15 minute
  jtv_percentage <-  react_pm %>%
    group_by(session, decision) %>%
    summarise(JTV_percentage_20min = round(100*sum(n_lapses)/sum(n_stimuli),2),
              JTV_percentage_last15min = round(100*sum(n_lapses[.$window>=6])/sum(n_stimuli[.$window>=6]), 2)) 
    
  # save overall JTv percentage 
  sm_jtv[[s]] <- jtv_percentage
  
  # save per minute lapses
  sm_pm_lapsing[[s]] <- react_pm
  
  
  ########################## Glasses blinks ########################
  # read glasses blink file
    smb_gl <- read.csv(list.files(paste0(sm_work_session[s],"/",sm_device[1]), pattern = "blink_correct_shape.csv", full.names = TRUE))
    smb_gl$device <- "Glasses"
    smb_gl$session <- basename(sm_work_session[s])
    # calculate window for each blinks belong to using start_time and end_time
    smb_gl$window <- 0
    for(t in 1:length(start_time))
      smb_gl[smb_gl$t_start > start_time[t] & smb_gl$t_end <= end_time[t],"window"] <- t-1
    
  
  ########################## SmartEye blinks ########################
  # read glasses blink file
    smb_sm <- read.csv(list.files(paste0(sm_work_session[s],"/",sm_device[2]), pattern = "blink_correct_shape.csv", full.names = TRUE))
    smb_sm$device <- "SmartEye"
    smb_sm$session <- basename(sm_work_session[s]) 
  
    # calculate window for each blinks belong to using start_time and end_time
    smb_sm$window <- 0
    for(t in 1:length(start_time))
      smb_sm[smb_sm$t_start > start_time[t] & smb_sm$t_end <= end_time[t],"window"] <- t-1
    
  #################### combine two device's data #######################
    sm_dt[[s]] <- rbind(smb_gl, smb_sm)
    sm_dt[[s]]$type <- factor(sm_dt[[s]]$type, level = c("1", "-1"))
    
     
   #################### obtain different stat: n_events, slope & Pearson correlation coefficient ####################### 
   
    statf <- 
       sm_dt[[s]] %>%
       group_by(session, device, type) %>%
       do(mod1 = lm(maxamp~maxvel, data = .), 
          mod2 = cor(.$maxamp, .$maxvel, method = "pearson"),
          mod3 = nrow(.)) %>%
       mutate(n_events = round(mod3[[1]],2),
              slope = round(summary(mod1)$coeff[2],2), 
              correlation = round(mod2[1],2)) %>%
       select(-mod1, -mod2, -mod3) %>%
       arrange(type)
      
     #### mean andstandard deviation of avr
     stat_avr <- 
       sm_dt[[s]] %>%
       group_by(session, device ,type) %>%
       summarise(mean_avr = round(mean(avr),3),
                 sd_avr = round(sd(avr),3)) %>% 
       arrange(type)
     
     statf <- right_join(statf, stat_avr, by=c("session", "device","type"))
     
     # store
     sm_stat[[s]] <- statf
     
 #################################### scatter plot ##############################
    
    sc_plot <-  
        ggplot(data=sm_dt[[s]], aes(x=maxvel, y =maxamp)) +
        geom_point(color = "blue", shape=21) + 
        facet_wrap(~ device+ type,  labeller = label_both )  +
        stat_smooth(method = "lm", col="red") + 
        ggtitle(basename(sm_session_in[s])) +
        theme_bw() +
        theme( plot.title = element_text(size=14, hjust = 0.5, face="bold"))
        
  
   #################################### table plot for stat ##############################  
   statdt <- data.frame(x=c(0,1),y=c(0,1))
   statplot <- ggplot(data=statdt, aes(x=x,y=y))+
               geom_blank()+
               annotation_custom(tableGrob( statf, rows =NULL, theme = ttheme_default(base_size = 10)), 
                                  xmin=0, xmax=1, ymin=0, ymax=1)+
                theme_bw()+
                theme(legend.position = "none",
                      axis.title.x=element_blank(),
                      axis.text.x=element_blank(),
                      axis.ticks.x=element_blank(),
                      axis.title.y=element_blank(),
                      axis.text.y=element_blank(),
                      axis.ticks.y=element_blank(),
                      panel.background = element_blank(),
                      panel.grid.major = element_blank(), 
                      panel.grid.minor = element_blank()) 
   
   
   #################################### table plot for JTV percentage ##############################  
   jtvdt <- data.frame(x=c(0,1),y=c(0,1))
   jtvplot <- ggplot(data=jtvdt, aes(x=x,y=y))+
               geom_blank()+
               annotation_custom(tableGrob( jtv_percentage, rows =NULL, theme = ttheme_default(base_size = 10)), 
                                  xmin=0, xmax=1, ymin=0, ymax=1)+
                theme_bw()+
                theme(legend.position = "none",
                      axis.title.x=element_blank(),
                      axis.text.x=element_blank(),
                      axis.ticks.x=element_blank(),
                      axis.title.y=element_blank(),
                      axis.text.y=element_blank(),
                      axis.ticks.y=element_blank(),
                      panel.background = element_blank(),
                      panel.grid.major = element_blank(), 
                      panel.grid.minor = element_blank())
   
   ######################## organise plots ####################
   
     grid.arrange(sc_plot, statplot, jtvplot,
             ncol=1,
             heights = c(7,2, 1))
     
  }

@





\section[Combined analysis]{Relationship betwen Amplitude and Velocity: combined sessions}


<<combine_data, cache=TRUE ,echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=

########################### combine for all subjects ####################

# jtv whole percetage
sm_jtv_all <- do.call(rbind, sm_jtv)

# jtv per minute lapsing
sm_jtv_pm_lapsing_all <- do.call(rbind,  sm_pm_lapsing)

# glasses & visage_smarteye data
sm_dt_all <- do.call(rbind, sm_dt)

# stat fro mboth devices
sm_stat_all <- do.call(rbind, sm_stat)


# add JTV_percentage_20min to the data
sm_dt_all <-  right_join(sm_dt_all, sm_jtv_all[,c("session", "JTV_percentage_20min")], by="session")

# add state JTV_percentage_20min > 5% ==> impaired,
sm_dt_all$state <- "not_impaired"
sm_dt_all$state[sm_dt_all$JTV_percentage_20min > 5] <- "impaired"


# add per minute n_lapses & n_stimuli with sm_dt_all
# remove if any NA occure due to coaortion
sm_dt_all <- na.omit(right_join(sm_dt_all , sm_jtv_pm_lapsing_all[,c("session","window", "n_stimuli", "n_lapses")], by=c("session", "window")))

@



<<final_Session_name, cache=TRUE ,echo=FALSE, warning=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=

########################### final sessions to build model #############################
# Drop sessions according to Murray's suggestions

sm_session_drop <- c("20160811_1130_pn", "20160812_0340_pn", "20160812_0440_pn", "20160817_041043_ms")
sm_session_final <- sm_session_in[! basename(sm_session_in) %in% sm_session_drop]


####################### final data #############################
sm_dt_final <- sm_dt_all[sm_dt_all$session %in% basename(sm_session_final), ]
sm_dt_final$state <- factor(sm_dt_final$state, level = c("not_impaired","impaired"))

########### number of final session ##########
n_session_final <- length(sm_session_final)

model_device <- "SmartEye"
@

After evaluating the relationship between amplitude and velocity, four more sessions were dropped from further analysis:

<<print_drop, cache=TRUE ,echo=FALSE, results='text'>>=
print(sm_session_drop)

@



Now we have \Sexpr{length(sm_session_final)} sessions, among which \Sexpr{length(unique(sm_dt_final$session[sm_dt_final$state=="not_impaired"]))} were `not\_impaired' and \Sexpr{length(unique(sm_dt_final$session[sm_dt_final$state=="impaired"]))} were `impaired'.


The relationship between amplitude and velocity is examined for all sessions combined and following comparison was carried: 

\begin{itemize}
\item comparison of relationship between maxamp and maxvel between two state of impairment. Within each state there are  the two devices and two types of events, eye closing and eye reopenning. Histograms of AVR are also produced in these four cases. A summary stat table shows the slope, correlation coefficients, mean of AVR, standard deviation of AVR in all four combinations.  

\item comparison of relationship between log(Positive ZCI)  and log(BTD)

\item comparison of relationship between log(Negative ZCI)  and log(BTD)

\item comparison of relationship between log(BECD)  and log(BTD)

\end{itemize}


These comparisons are taken place to examine the extent of relationship between variables in the `not\_impaired' and `impaired' subsets. The true state of impairment is obtained using the reaction time from JTV test output that was taken place simultenously during recordinds. Usually a cut-off percentage of lapsing ($5\%$) calcualted csidering whole recording session is used to label a subject's  state of impairment. If the total JTv percentage is below $5\%$ all observations in that sessions were labelled as `not\_impaired'.

However, it is also worth to mention that human brain can show some alertness during a drowsy period, and some signs of drowsyness during an alert period. Hence, no lapsing minute can be observed even though the JTV percentage from whole recording session was above the $5\%$ arbtrary thresh-holds. To make the algorithm accostom with these brain activity, the relationship between above mentioned variables are also examined in the conditional subset of data using per minute lapsing.    



